Complete Configuration Example Structure
Your main.tf should follow this pattern:

terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  required_providers {
    docker = {
      source  = "kreuzwerker/docker"
      version = "~> 3.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "docker" {}

resource "docker_network" "app_network" {
  name   = "terraform-network"
  driver = "bridge"
}

resource "docker_volume" "app_data" {
  name = var.volume_name
}

resource "docker_image" "nginx" {
  name = "nginx:latest"
}

resource "docker_container" "web_server" {
  name  = "terraform-nginx"
  image = docker_image.nginx.image_id
  
  ports {
    internal = 80
    external = var.web_port
  }
  
  networks_advanced {
    name = docker_network.app_network.name
  }
  
  volumes {
    volume_name    = docker_volume.app_data.name
    container_path = "/usr/share/nginx/html"
  }
  
  restart = var.restart_policy
}
Task 2: Apply Terraform Plan
Step 1: Initialize Terraform
# Initialize Terraform working directory
terraform init

# Expected output: Provider plugins downloaded
What happens during init:

Downloads required provider plugins
Creates .terraform directory
Initializes backend for state storage
Step 2: Validate Configuration
# Check configuration syntax
terraform validate

# Format configuration files
terraform fmt

# Review formatted files
cat main.tf
Step 3: Create Execution Plan
# Generate and review execution plan
terraform plan

# Save plan to file for review
terraform plan -out=tfplan

# Examine the plan details
terraform show tfplan
Review the plan output:

Resources to be created (green +)
Resources to be modified (yellow ~)
Resources to be destroyed (red -)
Step 4: Apply Configuration
# Apply the configuration
terraform apply

# Or apply saved plan
terraform apply tfplan

# Type 'yes' when prompted to confirm
Monitor the apply process:

Watch resource creation order
Note any dependencies being resolved
Verify successful completion message
Step 5: Verify Infrastructure
# Check Terraform state
terraform state list

# View specific resource details
terraform state show docker_container.web_server

# Verify Docker resources
docker ps
docker network ls
docker volume ls

# Test web server access
curl http://localhost:8080
Step 6: Inspect Outputs
# Display all outputs
terraform output

# Display specific output
terraform output web_url

# Output in JSON format
terraform output -json
Step 7: Modify Infrastructure
Create a terraform.tfvars file to customize variables:

# TODO: Set custom values for variables
volume_name    = "my-custom-volume"
web_port       = 8080
restart_policy = "unless-stopped"
Apply changes:

# Plan with new variables
terraform plan -var-file="terraform.tfvars"

# Apply changes
terraform apply -var-file="terraform.tfvars"
Step 8: Manage State
# List all resources in state
terraform state list

# Show state file location
ls -la terraform.tfstate

# View state in readable format
terraform show

# Refresh state from actual infrastructure
terraform refresh
Step 9: Destroy Infrastructure
# Preview destruction plan
terraform plan -destroy

# Destroy all managed infrastructure
terraform destroy

# Type 'yes' to confirm

# Verify cleanup
docker ps -a
docker network ls
docker volume ls
